{"records":[{"id":"wz6s96x2c4","title":"Rhinovirus triggers distinct host responses through differential engagement of epithelial innate immune signaling - Donor information","description":"Donor metadata of primary human nasal epithelial cells used in the study.\n\nIn this study:  We used single cell sequencing of the organotypic air-liquid interface culture model to reveal innate defense responses of the human nasal epithelium to rhinovirus, the most frequent cause of common colds and a major cause of serious respiratory illness in high-risk groups (e.g. smokers and asthmatics). We found that the epithelial interferon response restricts RV infection to <2% of cells. However, inhibiting the interferon response by blocking IRF3 activation increases the proportion of infected cells to >30%, and leads to a distinct NFKB- and NLRP1-dependent pro-inflammatory response, with production of neutrophil-attracting chemokines, increased mucus, and IL-1b. Inhibiting both IRF3 and NFKB increases cell death, ER stress and squamous metaplasia. Overall, this study demonstrates how epithelial innate immune signaling impacts RV-induced inflammation and reveals potential therapeutic targets. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-01-15T19:25:39.501Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/wz6s96x2c4","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/wz6s96x2c4"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Bao","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ellen","last_name":"Foxman","orcid":"0000-0001-9767-9942"}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/03v76x132","name":"Yale University","grant_id":null},{"id":"https://ror.org/0515k5w36","name":"Rita Allen Foundation","grant_id":null},{"id":"https://ror.org/043z4tv69","name":"National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases","grant_id":null}],"categories":["Metadata","Donor"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"n2cyh3pk4h","title":"Attenuated replication and damaging effects of SARS-CoV-2 Omicron variants in an intestinal epithelial barrier model","description":"Many COVID-19 patients suffer from gastrointestinal symptoms and impaired intestinal barrier function may play a key role in harmful inflammatory responses and Long COVID. Despite its importance, the impact of SARS-CoV-2 on intestinal epithelia is poorly understood. To address this, we established an intestinal barrier model integrating epithelial Caco-2 cells and mucus-secreting HT29 cells in the presence or absence of human Raji cells. We show that this gut epithelial model allows efficient differentiation of Caco-2 cells into M (microfold) cell-like cells and faithfully mimics intestinal barrier function. It is highly permissive to SARS-CoV-2 infection due to high expression of the viral entry factors ACE2 and TMPRSS2. Early strains of SARS-CoV-2 and the Delta variant replicated with high efficiency, severely disrupted barrier function and depleted tight junction proteins, such as claudin-1, occludin and ZO-1. In comparison, Omicron subvariants also depleted ZO-1 from tight junctions but had less damaging effects on mucosal integrity and barrier function. Remdesivir and the TMPRSS2 inhibitor Camostat prevented SARS-CoV-2 replication and thus epithelial barrier damage, while the Cathepsin inhibitor E64d was ineffective. Our results support that SARS-CoV-2 disrupts intestinal barrier function but further suggest that circulating Omicron variants are less damaging than earlier viral strains.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-02-16T14:22:56.963Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/n2cyh3pk4h","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/n2cyh3pk4h"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Meta","last_name":"Volcic","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Frank","last_name":"Kirchhoff","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rayhane","last_name":"Nchioua","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Chiara","last_name":"Pastorio","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Fabian","last_name":"Zech","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Clarissa","last_name":"Read","orcid":null},{"first_name":"paul","last_name":"walther","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","grant_id":"CRC1279"}],"categories":["Intestine","Tight Junction","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2","Intestinal Mucosal Barrier"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"8xvd7d874b","title":"Engineered Lactococcus lactis for Oral Delivery of Bovine Type I Interferon: A Biotherapeutic Strategy for Mucosal Antiviral Defense","description":"Interferons (IFNs) are key broad-spectrum antiviral cytokines and represent an important complement to conventional vaccination. However, the application of bovine interferons (BoIFNs) is limited by short biological half-life, poor in vivo stability, and inconvenient delivery. In this study, BoIFN-β1, BoIFN-β2, BoIFN-β3, and BoIFN-γ were expressed in Escherichia coli, and four fusion proteins (BoIFN-α-β2, BoIFN-α-γ, BoIFN-β2-γ, and BoIFN-α-β2-γ) were constructed to evaluate potential synergistic antiviral effects. All fusion proteins showed lower antiviral activity than BoIFN-α and BoIFN-β2 monomers. Subsequently, a food-grade pNZ8149/NZ3900 system was used to achieve antibiotic resistance marker-free secretory expression of BoIFN-α and BoIFN-β2 in Lactococcus lactis (L. lactis). Recombinant culture supernatants showed no apparent cytotoxicity toward Madin–Darby bovine kidney (MDBK) cells, effectively inhibited bovine enterovirus (BEV) and vesicular stomatitis virus (VSV) replication, and significantly induced MX1, OAS1, ISG15, and IFITM1 expression. In BALB/c mice, oral administration of recombinant L. lactis increased splenic Oas1 and Isg15 expression and reduced VSV-induced weight loss, tissue viral loads, and pathological lesions, with prophylactic administration showing greater efficacy. These findings establish a food-grade L. lactis-based oral BoIFN delivery strategy for bovine viral disease prevention and control.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-09-06T09:16:21.811Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/8xvd7d874b","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/8xvd7d874b"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Mingze","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"hongzhe","last_name":"Zhao","orcid":"0000-0002-6654-9923"},{"first_name":"shilong","last_name":"You","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Probiotics","Antiviral Therapeutics","Interferon Immunotherapy"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"4kwg2ydz2g","title":"Decitabine activates type I interferon signaling to inhibit p53-deficient myeloid malignant cells","description":"Raw data of manuscript entitled \"Decitabine activates type I interferon signaling to inhibit p53-deficient myeloid malignant cells\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-05-28T20:24:20.498Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/4kwg2ydz2g","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/4kwg2ydz2g"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jiale","last_name":"Wu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yuntong","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jiaqi","last_name":"Wu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Huaxin","last_name":"Song","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yigang","last_name":"Tang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ni","last_name":" Yan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lili","last_name":"Wu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sujiang","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"ChunKang","last_name":"Chang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Min","last_name":"Lu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","Acute Myelogenous Leukemia","Protein P53"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"x7hw356g4d","title":"Confocal imaging and microscale thermophoresis dataset from the article: \"A type I interferon regulatory network for human plasmacytoid dendritic cells based on heparin, membrane-bound and soluble type II C-type lectin BDCA-2\"","description":"Confocal imaging and microscale thermophoresis dataset from the article: A novel type I interferon regulatory network for human plasmacytoid dendritic cells based on heparin, membrane-bound and soluble type II C-type lectin BDCA-2.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-03-08T10:34:44.714Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/x7hw356g4d","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/x7hw356g4d"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Francisco","last_name":"Venegas","orcid":"0000-0001-5799-8711"},{"first_name":"Philipp","last_name":"Yu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Andreas ","last_name":"Kaufmann","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sven-A.","last_name":"Freibert","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Stefan","last_name":"Bauer","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Immunology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"324cs9ymc2","title":"Evasion of type-I interferon by SARS-CoV-2","description":"The files include the raw data for Western blots and IFA images in the manuscript \"Evasion of type-I interferon by SARS-CoV-2\".","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-09-25T07:50:41.492Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/324cs9ymc2","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/324cs9ymc2"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Hongjie","last_name":"Xia","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot","Fluorescence Imaging"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"gd4br4v6jj","title":"African swine fever virus pMGF360 12L antagonizes type I interferon signaling by degrading IRF9 via the proteasome pathway","description":"African swine fever virus pMGF360 12L antagonizes type I interferon signaling by degrading IRF9 via the proteasome pathway Data set.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-10-22T15:41:34.047Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gd4br4v6jj","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gd4br4v6jj"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"z","last_name":"H","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virus","Innate Immunity"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"ykn8g3grwv","title":"IFN-I Treatment Letter Supplementary Materials","description":"Supplementary material for a manuscript entitled \"Type I interferon treatment and risk of granuloma annulare & lichen planus: A global cohort study ,\" submitted as a research letter to IJD.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-06-30T04:32:06.012Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ykn8g3grwv","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ykn8g3grwv"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Brandon","last_name":"Block","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Dermatology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"spgdgkwbtw","title":"Truncating NFKB1 variants cause combined NLRP3 inflammasome activation and type I interferon signaling and predispose to necrotizing fasciitis","description":"Cell Reports Medicine article: Truncating NFKB1 variants cause combined NLRP3 inflammasome activation and type I interferon signaling and predispose to necrotizing fasciitis original blot and flow cytometry data.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-04-08T15:08:45.043Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/spgdgkwbtw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/spgdgkwbtw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Katariina","last_name":"Nurmi","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Autophagy","NFKB1 Transcription Factor","NLRP3 Inflammasome"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"5w7rt5mxxp","title":"Metabolic enzyme UAP1 mediates IRF3 pyrophosphorylation to facilitate innate immune response.Yang et al","description":"Post-translational modifications (PTMs) of proteins are crucial to guarantee the proper biological functions in immune responses. Although protein phosphorylation has been extensively studied, our current knowledge of protein pyrophosphorylation, which occurs based on phosphorylation, is very limited. Protein pyrophosphorylation is originally considered to be a non-enzymatic process, and its function in immune signaling is unknown. Here we identify a metabolic enzyme, UDP-N-acetylglucosamine pyrophosphorylase 1 (UAP1) as a pyrophosphorylase for protein serine pyrophosphorylation, by catalyzing the pyrophosphorylation of interferon regulatory factor 3 (IRF3) at serine (Ser) 386 to promote robust type I interferon (IFN) responses. Uap1 deficiency significantly impairs the activation of both DNA and RNA viruses-induced type I IFN pathways, and the Uap1 deficient mice are highly susceptible to lethal viral infection. Our findings demonstrate protein pyrophosphorylation as a novel type of PTM in the regulation of antiviral responses, and provide insights into the crosstalk between metabolism and innate immunity. \n\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-11-28T18:31:16.45Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/5w7rt5mxxp","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/5w7rt5mxxp"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"shuai","last_name":"Yang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"JUN","last_name":"CUI","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":122176,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":161,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":122176,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":121632,"restricted":64,"embargoed":480},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2092},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Esta